> * Previously, spellcasters' offensive spells were limited to Open
> Wounds, Psi Bolt, Geyser, and some elemental attacks. Now you'll get
> pelted with acid rain, boulders, foul sludge, sleep rays and a few
> other things.

Interesting, although to discuss them we'd have to know why they're
important from a game standpoint.  Does acid rain damage items?  What
does foul sludge do?

> * And, there are spells designed for incapacitation, such as Punish,
> Earthquake and Make Web.

Ah, a Punishment spell could be interesting.  How does Earthquake work,
does it harm dungeon walls ala a Drum of Earthquake?

> * Summon Nasties and Curse Items are, incidentally, less common.

These are two of the "big" spells, but I'd rate variety over difficulty
here.

> * Touch of Death has been replaced with Turn to Stone, which starts the
> "slow" petrification process.

This is important: does the typical means of resisting Touch of Death
(magic resistance) work against Turn to Stone?  Remember that turning
to stone starts out by destroying a player's natural speed, although
you can get that back quickly and permanently by simply expending a
charge out of a wand of Speed Monster.  Interesting.  It's a little sad
to get rid of the sole benefit of hallucination, but overall I like the
change, as it reduces the statis of magic resistance from Absolutely
Essential to merely Highly Recommended.  Now if there were just some
other way of avoiding armor destruction from polymorph traps and
Destroy Armor spells....

Death should retain his Touch of Death attack, of course.


> * A few spells have stronger effects if they're cast by a ruling
> monster (a high priest, a demon prince or the Wizard.)

Interesting idea.


> This patch is extremely subject to change, especially if someone can
> find a breach in game balance (or program stability) somewhere.

Sounds like a good start, best of luck to you!

